# function boom() { throw new RangeError("r"); }
# function f() { using r0 = boom(); return 0; }
# try { print(f()); } catch (e) { print(e.name); }
# f1 = boom, f2 = f, f4 = the function of the library that finds the dispose method of what `using` holds. It has no function object: the walk must enter it directly.
# Link makes a call only when its operands have types, and what never comes back gives none: nobody else calls f4, so it is not reached and the plan has no unit of it to enter.
# RUN(LINK): link functions mechanisms=all
# LINK: function f4 unreached
# boom() never returns by its own text, so the block ends behind its call and the walk does not arrive at the helper's. It was an internal compiler error once.
# RUN: walk f2 mechanisms=all
# CHECK-LABEL: code f2:Body
# CHECK: = CallDirect {{*}}function=f1
# CHECK-NEXT: Unreachable
# RUN(RUNS): run-in-shell mechanisms=all
# RUNS: RangeError
# RUNS-NEXT: exited with 0

